        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How We Chose These Massage Chairs</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these chairs. They were chosen from makers' product pages and
            manuals, published delivery and warranty terms, recall records and independent coverage,
            and the claims on this page are limited to what those sources support.
          </p>
          <p>
            We started from brands that sell full-body zero gravity chairs on Amazon or through
            major US retailers — Osaki and Titan (both owned by OTA World, as Garage Gym Reviews notes),
            Real Relax, Bob and Brad, Human Touch, Kahuna and Luraco — and checked what each maker's
            US store listed on 19 October 2026. We then looked for an independent source for each
            chair's track, height and weight limits, dimensions and warranty. Garage Gym Reviews has
            tested the Bob and Brad and Human Touch chairs; BarBend has tested the Human Touch;
            Brookstone and other retailers list the Osaki Champ II; and the Bob and Brad Amazon
            listing could be read through search results.
          </p>
          <p>
            Where figures conflicted we left them off the card. The most common conflicts were
            maximum user weight (Human Touch lists 285 lb, one reviewer 300 lb; Real Relax lists 120
            kg, about 265 lb, while several roundup sites print 400 lb), dimensions (Human Touch's
            own web page and manual give different upright lengths) and warranty (Bob and Brad's
            Amazon listing says one year, an independent review says two).
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Safety screening.</strong> We searched the CPSC recall database by recall title,
            manufacturer and recall description for Bob and Brad, Osaki, Titan, OTA, Real Relax,
            Human Touch, Kahuna, Luraco, Bodyfriend, Synca and Costway, and by product type for
            massage chairs and massagers. We found no recall of any massage chair carded here. Human
            Touch has two older recalls on record, both for its Perfect Chair recliners rather than
            massage chairs: a 2009 repair for an entanglement hazard in the screw drive and a 2016
            recall of Perfect power recliners for a fall hazard. Costway appears for recalls of baby
            products and, in 2026, portable steam saunas, not massage chairs. The massager recalls in
            the database — HoMedics massagers in 2017 and 2024, and heated massage recliners sold at
            Value City Furniture in 2007 — involve products not listed here.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What we left out.</strong> Costway's Therapy 22 is well reviewed by both Garage
            Gym Reviews and BarBend, which agree on its dimensions and three-year warranty, but we
            could not load Costway's own page to confirm it is still sold. Kahuna lists its Dios-6800
            at $3,799 with a 50 in. SL track, but we found no independent source for its figures.
            Luraco's US-assembled chairs are well regarded at the premium end, and Bodyfriend sells
            in the US mainly through its own showrooms; we could not check either chair's current
            figures on the day. Synca's compact CirC is described as having an L-track and an
            SL-track in the same review, so we could not say which. Each is mentioned here rather
            than carded.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="zero-gravity" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What Zero Gravity Actually Means</h2>
<p>
            "Zero gravity" in a massage chair is a reclined position with the legs raised to roughly
            the level of the heart or slightly above, so the body's weight is spread along the back
            rather than concentrated on the seat. Makers borrow the name from the posture astronauts
            adopt at launch. In practice it does two things: it presses your back more firmly into
            the rollers, which most people experience as a deeper massage, and it is a comfortable
            position to lie in for a quarter of an hour.
          </p>
          <p>
            Most current full-body chairs have it. Osaki and Titan describe three stages of zero
            gravity; Bob and Brad lists a 166° recline. A higher number of stages or degrees does not
            make one chair better than another; what matters is whether the reclined position is
            comfortable for your height and whether the chair can reach it in your room.
          </p>
          <p>
            Zero gravity is a comfort position, not a treatment. Claims that it decompresses the spine,
            improves circulation or relieves back pain are makers' marketing, and they are reported
            here as such. If you have a back condition, the recline itself — not just the massage — is
            something to ask your doctor about.
          </p>
          <p>
            The one practical downside is space. A chair that reclines fully needs room behind it, in
            front of it or both, and "space-saving" designs solve that by sliding the seat forward as
            the back reclines, so the chair needs only a few inches behind it but more in front. That
            is the subject of the <a href="#space" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">space section</a> below.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tracks-and-rollers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>S, L and SL Tracks, and 2D, 3D and 4D Rollers</h2>
<p>
            Two sets of jargon describe how the back massage works: the track the rollers run on, and
            how many ways the rollers can move.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Track type</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>S-track</strong> follows the S-shaped curve of the spine from the neck to the
              lower back. It contours well but stops at the waist.</li>
            <li><strong>L-track</strong> runs from the neck, down the back and under the seat to the
              glutes and the top of the hamstrings, but follows the spine's curve less closely.</li>
            <li><strong>SL-track</strong> combines the two: an S-shaped back section that continues
              under the seat. It is now standard on full-body chairs, and every zero gravity chair on
              this page has one.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Track length is quoted in inches, from about 34 in. on compact chairs to 50 in. and more
            on full-size ones; Bob and Brad's Amazon listing gives 51.4 in. and Kahuna lists 50 in. A longer track covers more
            of a tall user's back and glutes, but only if the chair also positions the rollers well
            for your shoulder height, which is what "body scan" features try to do.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">2D, 3D and 4D</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>2D</strong> rollers move up and down the track and side to side.</li>
            <li><strong>3D</strong> adds in-and-out movement, so the rollers can press deeper or
              lighter. Most mid-priced chairs, including the Osaki Champ II, Real Relax Favor-06 and
              Human Touch WholeBody 8.0, are sold as 3D.</li>
            <li><strong>4D</strong> adds control over speed and rhythm, which makers describe as closer
              to a human hand. Bob and Brad's and Titan's chairs here are 4D.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            These are marketing categories rather than standards, and two 3D chairs can feel very
            different. Reviewers consistently say the best way to judge intensity is to sit in the
            chair, and that the strongest setting on a premium chair can be too firm for some people.
            If you can, try a chair in a showroom before you buy, and check the return terms if you
            cannot.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Airbags, heat and the rest</h3>
          <p>
            Airbags squeeze the arms, hips, calves and feet; foot rollers work the soles; lumbar heat
            warms the lower back. Bluetooth speakers, app control, voice commands and "health
            detection" sensors appear on many chairs. They add to the price and the list of things
            that can break; decide which ones you would actually use.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Height, Weight and Body Fit</h2>
<p>
            A chair that does not fit your body gives a poor massage however good its mechanism is.
            Check three things.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Maximum user weight.</strong> On this page the limits we could confirm run from
            about 264 lb (Osaki Champ II, Titan Astro) to 265 lb (Bob and Brad). Titan's manual adds
            that heavier users may make the chair noisier and wear the fabric faster. Where makers
            and other sources disagreed — Human Touch and Real Relax — we left the figure off the card;
            ask the seller for the current figure in writing if you are near the limit.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>User height.</strong> Few makers publish a height range at all. Bob and Brad says
            up to 6 ft 3 in. Most chairs adjust to height through a body scan that sets the shoulder
            position and an extending footrest — Osaki lists up to 8.5 in. of footrest extension on the
            Champ II and Titan up to 8 in. on the Astro. Very tall users often find the rollers stop
            short of the top of the shoulders or the calf section ends mid-shin; very short users find
            their feet do not reach the foot rollers. If you are much taller or shorter than average,
            ask the seller or try before you buy.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Shoulder and seat width.</strong> Full-body chairs wrap the shoulders and hips
            with airbags. Osaki lists shoulder width of 23 in. on the Champ II and Real Relax 21.26 in.
            with the airbags deflated on the Favor-06. Broad-shouldered users sometimes find the arm
            and shoulder airbags uncomfortably tight; most chairs let you turn the airbag intensity
            down or off.
          </p>
          <p>
            If two people of very different sizes will share the chair, that matters more than any
            single feature. A chair with a body scan, a long extending footrest and adjustable airbag
            pressure suits a household better than one tuned for a single body.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="space" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Footprint, Wall Clearance and Doorways</h2>
<p>
            This is the section to read with a tape measure. Three measurements decide whether a chair
            works in your room.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Reclined length</h3>
          <p>
            Massage chairs grow when they recline. Osaki lists the Champ II at 59 in. long upright and
            71 in. reclined; Titan lists the Astro at 62.2 in. upright and 72 in. reclined; Human Touch
            lists the WholeBody 8.0 at 66 in. reclined. Plan for the reclined length plus room to get
            in and out, and remember the footrest extension adds more for tall users.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Wall clearance</h3>
          <p>
            Space-saving chairs slide forward as they recline, so they need only a few inches behind
            them. The makers' figures vary, and the figures from a single maker do not always agree.
            Osaki says the Champ II can sit 7 in. from the wall. Titan's product page says the Astro
            needs 4 in., but its manual's installation diagram says at least 7 cm (under 3 in.) behind
            the backrest and at least 50 cm in front. Human Touch lists 9 in. of rear recline clearance
            for the WholeBody 8.0 in both its web specifications and its manual. Real Relax's FAQ
            recommends at least 50 cm (about 20 in.) behind the Favor-06 and 30 cm in front, and 1 m
            from TVs or audio equipment to avoid interference. When figures conflict, use the larger
            one.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Doorways, stairs and the delivery route</h3>
          <p>
            Measure every door, hallway corner and stairwell between the truck and the room. Osaki
            gives a minimum door width of 29.5 in. for the Champ II (Brookstone's listing says 30 in.)
            and explains how to measure it: the narrowest inner width of the frame, minus an allowance
            for clearance. Chairs ship in one or two boxes weighing roughly 145 to 225 lb on the
            figures makers publish, and many are delivered only to the curb or the first floor. Our <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to treadmill space requirements</a> explains how to measure a room and a delivery route for a large machine, and the same
            method works here.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The floor.</strong> A chair of 114 to 213 lb plus an adult is a concentrated load
            on four feet or a base. That is not unusual for domestic furniture, but put felt pads or a
            mat under it on a wood floor, and if the floor is old, unusual or already loaded with
            other heavy kit, ask a qualified professional.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="delivery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Delivery, Assembly and Moving a Massage Chair</h2>
<p>
            Delivery terms decide a lot of the real cost, and they vary more than the chairs do.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Curbside or to the door.</strong> Osaki's store offered free curbside
              delivery for its Osaki and Titan chairs on the day; Human Touch offered free delivery to the front door, first
              floor only. Curbside means you and some strong friends move a box of 200 lb or more up
              the drive and into the house.</li>
            <li><strong>White glove.</strong> Osaki charged $275 and Human Touch $349 for white-glove
              delivery, which Human Touch defines as delivery to the first floor, assembly in the
              room of your choice and removal of the packaging. Ask whether stairs are included; often
              they cost extra or are excluded.</li>
            <li><strong>Amazon and marketplace sellers.</strong> Terms depend on the seller and the
              carrier. Garage Gym Reviews says its Bob and Brad chair was brought inside by the
              delivery team, which did not assemble it.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            <strong>Assembly</strong> is usually modest: attach the footrest or leg section, connect
            wiring and air hoses, fit the side panels and the remote holder. Garage Gym Reviews' tester
            says the Bob and Brad chair took about five minutes, and a Real Relax owner review
            describes attaching only the footrest. The hard part is lifting the chair out of its box.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Moving it later.</strong> Most chairs have wheels at the back and tilt onto them,
            but Garage Gym Reviews notes that its 213 lb Bob and Brad chair needs two people to move.
            The lifting principles in our guide to <a href="/how-to-move-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">moving a treadmill</a> apply: unplug it, remove what comes off, protect the floor, and do not carry it down
            stairs on your own.
          </p>
          <p>
            Keep the box if you have room until the return window closes. Several sellers require the <a href="/how-to-pack-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>original packaging</a> for a return, and return shipping on a 200 lb freight item is rarely
            free; check the current terms before you order, because they change.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="power-and-warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Power, Warranty and the Price You Actually Pay</h2>
<h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Power</h3>
          <p>
            Every chair here runs on a standard 110 to 120 V household outlet. They draw little
            power — Real Relax lists 100 W for the Favor-06 and Human Touch 150 W for the WholeBody
            8.0, less than a typical treadmill motor — so a dedicated circuit is not normally needed.
            What the manuals do insist on is grounding: Human Touch's manual says the chair must be
            plugged into a properly installed and grounded outlet and not modified to fit another,
            and Titan's manual says the same about the earth connection. Do not use an ungrounded
            adapter, keep the cord out of the recline mechanism, and do not use the chair with wet
            skin or on a damp floor. If you are tempted to run it from an extension cord, our guide
            to <a href="/treadmill-extension-cord/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">extension cords and treadmills</a> explains why treadmill makers advise against them, and the same reasoning applies to a
            grounded appliance of this size.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Warranty</h3>
          <p>
            Read the structure, not just the headline number. Osaki's store offered, for both its
            Osaki and Titan chairs, one year of parts and labor plus parts only in years two and
            three, with paid extensions; Human Touch
            includes a three-year limited warranty with a paid upgrade to in-home service; Real Relax
            lists one year. In-home service matters, because the alternative is shipping a 200 lb chair
            to a repair center. Bob and Brad's warranty term differs between its Amazon listing and an
            independent review, so confirm it with the seller.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Price</h3>
          <p>
            Massage chair prices are the least stable figures in this series. On 19 October the same
            Titan Pro 4D Astro was $2,799 on Osaki's store and $4,499 on Titan's, both showing a
            higher "compare at" price; the Osaki Champ II was $3,999 at Osaki and $4,999 at
            Brookstone; Human Touch's WholeBody 8.0 was $1,999 at Human Touch against $2,399 and
            $2,499 in two independent reviews. Large permanent discounts are common, so treat a
            crossed-out price as marketing, compare at least two stores, and include delivery and the
            warranty upgrade in the total.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-not" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Should Not Use a Massage Chair</h2>
<p>
            Massage chairs press hard on the spine, ribs, shoulders and legs, and several add heat.
            The makers' manuals are explicit about who should not use them.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Titan's Pro 4D Astro manual</strong> (Titan and Osaki are both OTA World brands)
            says people with the following should not use the chair, or should use it only on a
            doctor's advice: osteoporosis, vertebral fractures or acute sprains and strains; heart
            disease, including pacemakers or other implanted electronics; cancer; reduced sensation
            from circulatory problems such as diabetes; an abnormal or curved spine or a herniated
            disc; pregnancy or recent childbirth; thrombosis, acute varicose veins or skin
            inflammation; inability to sense temperature; and high fever or acute illness. It also
            says the chair is not for people over 80, for children or for anyone unable to operate it,
            not to fall asleep in it, and that the maximum suggested session is 20 minutes.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Real Relax</strong> says in its Favor-06 FAQ that it does not recommend the chair
            for people aged 80 or older or for those with serious chronic back or spinal conditions
            such as severe osteoporosis, herniated discs or recent spinal surgery, and to consult a
            healthcare provider with any specific concerns. Its auto programs stop after 15 minutes. <strong>Human Touch's</strong> manual tells users to stop and consult a physician if they
            feel any abnormal discomfort, and that children and people with reduced physical, sensory
            or mental capabilities need supervision.
          </p>
          <p>
            As general guidance only: if any of those apply to you, or you are recovering from an
            injury or surgery, ask your doctor before buying. Keep sessions short, start on the
            gentlest setting, do not use heat over numb skin, and stop for pain, tingling, dizziness
            or anything that feels wrong. Keep children and pets away from the footrest and the gap
            between the seat and leg section while the chair moves.
          </p>
          <p>
            The same caution applies to the other powered recovery tools in this series; our guides
            to <a href="/best-compression-boots/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">compression boots</a> and <a href="/best-vibration-plates/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">vibration plates</a> carry their own makers' contraindication lists.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Should Buy Something Else</h2>
<p>
            <strong>If you mainly want to treat sore legs after training</strong>, a massage chair is
            an expensive way to do it. A <a href="/best-massage-guns/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">massage gun</a>,
            a <a href="/best-foam-rollers/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">foam roller</a> or a pair of compression boots reaches the hamstrings, quads and calves more directly for
            a fraction of the price.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If your room is small or your stairs are narrow</strong>, measure first, and
            consider a compact recliner such as the Human Touch WholeBody 8.0 or simply a good
            recliner and a handheld massager.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you have a spinal, bone or heart condition</strong>, ask your doctor before you
            spend money, and consider whether a session with a qualified massage therapist, who can
            adapt to your condition, is the safer choice.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you are buying for an older parent</strong>, check the makers' age guidance —
            Titan and Real Relax both advise against use over 80 — and think about getting in and out
            of a chair that reclines almost flat. Our guide to <a href="/best-treadmills-for-seniors/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills for seniors</a> covers the wider point about choosing equipment for older users.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you want recovery as part of a home gym</strong>, our <a href="/home-workout-equipment-reviews/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home workout equipment reviews</a> put the money in context: a massage chair costs as much as a rack, bench, bar and plates,
            or a good treadmill.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying a Massage Chair</h2>
          <p>Each of these is easy to avoid before you order and expensive to fix once a 200 lb box is on the porch.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Not measuring the delivery route</h3>
              <p>A chair that will not fit through a door, round a hallway corner or up a stairwell is a freight return. Measure the narrowest point between the truck and the room against the maker's minimum doorway figure, check box dimensions, and ask whether delivery includes stairs before you pay.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Forgetting the reclined length</h3>
              <p>The upright footprint is not the one that matters. Chairs grow by a foot or more when reclined, and space-saving designs need room in front even if they need little behind. Use the larger of any conflicting clearance figures, and mark the reclined outline on the floor with tape before ordering.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Ignoring the weight and height limits</h3>
              <p>A chair used near or above its user limit is noisier, wears faster and may not position the rollers well. Check the limit for everyone who will use it, ask the seller to confirm in writing if figures conflict, and be wary of roundup sites that print much higher limits than the maker.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Skipping the contraindications</h3>
              <p>Massage chair manuals list conditions, from osteoporosis and disc problems to pacemakers, pregnancy and clots, that rule out use or need a doctor's approval. Read the list before you buy, keep sessions to the maker's suggested length, and stop for pain, numbness or dizziness.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">For most homes, buy the Bob and Brad iMaster Pro</strong>: its
            limits, size and recline are the best documented of any chair here, and it is sold on
            Amazon. Choose the Osaki Champ II for a tight room and white-glove delivery, the Real Relax
            Favor-06 if budget rules and you are well inside its limits, the Titan Pro 4D Endor if you
            want 4D on Amazon and will confirm its user limits first, and the Human Touch WholeBody 8.0 if you do not need zero
            gravity at all.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whatever you buy, measure the room and the route, read the manual's contraindications, and
            compare stores, because prices swing by hundreds of dollars. If you mainly want to recover
            from training, start with <a href="/best-massage-guns/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">a massage gun</a> or the other tools in our <a href="/best-recovery-tools-for-weightlifters/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">recovery tools guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

