        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What is the best zero gravity massage chair?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most homes, the Bob and Brad iMaster Pro, at about $3,300 when we checked, because its 4D mechanism, 51.4 inch SL track, 166 degree recline and 6 ft 3 in, 265 lb limits are confirmed by the maker, an independent review and its Amazon listing. The Osaki Champ II suits tight rooms; the Real Relax Favor-06 is the budget choice.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a zero gravity massage chair worth it?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It can be, if you will use it most days and it fits your body and room. Zero gravity is a comfortable reclined position that presses your back into the rollers; it is not a treatment. If your goal is sore legs after training, a massage gun, foam roller or compression boots cost far less. Claims of pain relief are the makers' marketing.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the difference between an S, L and SL track?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              An S-track follows the curve of the spine from neck to lower back. An L-track runs from the neck under the seat to the glutes and hamstrings but contours the spine less closely. An SL-track combines both, and is standard on full-body chairs. Track length, from about 34 to over 50 inches, decides how much of a tall user's back is covered.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much space does a zero gravity massage chair need?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Plan for the reclined length, about 66 to 72 inches for the chairs here, plus room to get in and out. Space-saving chairs need only a few inches behind them, but makers' figures vary and sometimes conflict between a product page and its manual, so use the larger figure. Measure doorways too; Osaki gives about 30 inches for the Champ II.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Who should not use a massage chair?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Makers' manuals list osteoporosis, vertebral fractures, herniated discs and spinal deformity, heart disease or a pacemaker, cancer, thrombosis or acute varicose veins, pregnancy or recent childbirth, reduced sensation from diabetes, skin inflammation and acute illness. Titan and Real Relax advise against use over age 80. Ask a doctor first if any apply, and keep sessions short.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do massage chairs need a special outlet?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Not usually. The chairs here run on a standard 110 to 120 volt household outlet and draw around 100 to 150 watts. Their manuals do require a properly grounded outlet, warn against modifying the plug or using adapters, and advise against use with wet skin or on a damp floor. Avoid running a chair from an extension cord.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

